digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56873-z
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The study aims to develop and evaluate the rationally designed APR-based peptide P33, derived from an aggregation-prone segment of E. coli RhtA, as a broad-spectrum anti-ESKAPE antimicrobial by testing whether it induces aggregation of conserved transmembrane and ribosomal proteins, disrupts bacterial proteostasis, and limits resistance emergence.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When an urgent resistance threat is paired with a mechanistically motivated, multi-target antimicrobial peptide candidate that perturbs conserved protein-folding or proteostasis processes, the appropriate paper-level aim is to develop and evaluate that candidate for broad-spectrum antibacterial activity and resistance resilience."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Antimicrobial peptides are a plausible antibacterial development platform because they can show fast bactericidal activity and diverse mechanisms, including intracellular targeting with therapeutic potential against drug-resistant microbes."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Multi-target APR-based antibacterial peptides are expected to reduce simple resistance escape and promote proteostatic collapse when they target many proteins or conserved low-mutation APR regions."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Drug-resistant bacterial pathogens constitute a serious high-mortality threat because they are associated with large mortality burdens and can evade commonly used antibiotics through multidrug-resistance mechanisms."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) P33 is an APR-based peptide candidate derived from the transmembrane region of E. coli RhtA, making it relevant to APR-targeting antibacterial design."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Because P33 killing depends on protein aggregation, P33 motivates mechanistic investigation of aggregation-related antimicrobial peptide activity."];
  "ROOT" [label="(0,0,0) FINAL RESEARCH IDEA: Propose and execute a study to (i) mechanistically characterize P33 as an APR-based, aggregation-inducing antimicrobial peptide that preferentially aggregates conserved transmembrane (membrane-homeostasis) and ribosomal proteins, and (ii) test/validate its conserved broad-spectrum antibacterial activity across ESKAPE pathogens as a resistance-resilient antibiotic approach."];
  "n1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently, drug-resistant pathogens have become a major global health challenge."];
  "n10" [label="(11,0,0) Currently, AMP mechanisms are diverse and can include intracellular targeting, which can enhance their therapeutic potential against drug-resistant microbes."];
  "n15" [label="(15,0,0) Currently, most proteins have at least one APR, and APR targeting can be highly specific."];
  "n16" [label="(16,0,0) Currently, APRs are often in hydrophobic cores or transmembrane regions where mutation rates are intrinsically low."];
  "n17" [label="(19,0,0) Currently, an alternative antibacterial aggregating-peptide design is to choose APRs that occur in multiple proteins so that multiple proteins are targeted simultaneously."];
  "n18" [label="(20,0,0) Currently, multi-target APR designs aim to reduce the impact of single resistance-conferring mutations and trigger widespread aggregation leading to proteostatic collapse."];
  "n19" [label="(0,0,0) If an antibacterial peptide targets conserved/low-mutation APRs and/or many proteins at once, then resistance via single-point mutation is less likely and proteostasis collapse is more likely, supporting a broad-spectrum, resistance-resilient antibiotic strategy."];
  "n2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently, drug-resistant bacteria evade commonly used antibiotics via diverse multidrug-resistance mechanisms."];
  "n21" [label="(21,0,0) Currently, because bacterial membrane homeostasis is a valuable broad-spectrum target, the study explores whether disrupting membrane protein folding can yield broad-spectrum activity against ESKAPE pathogens."];
  "n22" [label="(0,0,0) If membrane homeostasis is a valuable broad-spectrum target, then inducing misfolding/aggregation of membrane (transmembrane) proteins is a plausible way to disrupt membrane homeostasis and achieve broad-spectrum antibacterial effects."];
  "n23" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently membrane homeostasis is a valuable broad-spectrum target, and if so then inducing misfolding/aggregation of transmembrane proteins could disrupt it and yield broad-spectrum effects; so misfolding/aggregation of transmembrane proteins is a plausible route to broad-spectrum antibacterial activity."];
  "n24" [label="(22,0,0) Currently, the researchers choose peptide P33, previously identified in an E. coli screen using randomly selected APRs, for this membrane-folding-disruption purpose."];
  "n25" [label="(23,0,0) Currently, the screen’s selection principle was to pick APRs with maximal homologous fragments across many proteins, aiming to aggregate multiple proteins at once and cause protein-homeostasis failure."];
  "n26" [label="(24,0,0) Currently, P33 was a potent hit and is based on an APR from the transmembrane region of E. coli RhtA."];
  "n3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently, major drug-resistant pathogens are linked to very large mortality (e.g., millions of deaths in 2019)."];
  "n30" [label="(28,0,0) Currently, E. coli shows little/no resistance build-up to P33 (even in a hypermutator strain), consistent with a multi-target mode of action."];
  "n31" [label="(29,0,0) Currently, P33’s mechanism of action depends on protein aggregation."];
  "n32" [label="(44,0,0) Currently, the study discovered P33 aggregates many proteins, with bias toward transmembrane proteins and ribosomal components."];
  "n33" [label="(45,0,0) Currently, screening across other ESKAPE pathogens with conserved targets found broad-spectrum activity of P33 with a conserved mode of action."];
  "n34" [label="(0,0,0) If P33 induces aggregation of many proteins (especially conserved transmembrane/ribosomal components) and shows broad-spectrum activity with conserved mechanism across ESKAPE, then P33 is a strong candidate lead to study mechanistically and to evaluate as a broad-spectrum anti-ESKAPE approach."];
  "n35" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently P33 aggregates many proteins with TM/ribosome bias and shows conserved broad-spectrum activity across ESKAPE, and if so then it is a strong lead to study and evaluate; so P33 should be investigated for mode of action and broad-spectrum anti-ESKAPE utility."];
  "n36" [label="(0,0,0) If a key open question is whether amyloid/aggregation-related AMP behaviors contribute to antimicrobial action, then observing antimicrobial peptides whose killing depends on aggregation motivates mechanistic investigation of aggregation (including possible amyloid-like inclusions) as the causal basis of antibacterial activity."];
  "n38" [label="(0,0,0) If there is an urgent need for new anti-ESKAPE approaches and aggregation/APR-based peptides can disrupt proteostasis (including TM protein folding) with multi-target, resistance-resilient potential, then a reasonable research proposal is to develop and test an APR-based aggregating peptide (P33) as a broad-spectrum anti-ESKAPE antibiotic by characterizing its aggregation-driven mechanism and assessing activity across ESKAPE."];
  "n39" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently there is urgent need for new anti-ESKAPE strategies and aggregation/APR-based, multi-target TM-disrupting peptides appear plausible; and if so then the research should focus on mechanistic and broad-spectrum evaluation of P33; so the core research proposal is to characterize and validate P33 as a broad-spectrum anti-ESKAPE, aggregation-driven antibiotic strategy."];
  "n5" [label="(0,0,0) If a pathogen group is a major, high-mortality antibiotic-resistance threat and commonly evades current antibiotics, then new effective antibiotic approaches against it are urgently needed."];
  "n6" [label="(5,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently drug-resistant pathogens pose a large threat, and if such threats evade current antibiotics then new approaches are urgently needed, so new approaches to develop effective antibiotics are urgently needed."];
  "n9" [label="(8,0,0) Currently, researchers have developed natural and synthetic AMPs valued for fast, efficient bactericidal activity."];
